{:verdict :revise
 :sounds-like-game "Partly: the estate places (shelf, porch leash, worm jar, front-office folder) and the '!' framing feel like Prime Meridian, but the voice is mostly a polite office status report with none of the jack-in/jack-out shorthand, and several facts break the GDD."
 :findings
 [{:severity :blocker
   :where ":clerk-1 :body"
   :issue "The Clerk's counts are wrong. It says 'two worker notes (one blueprint, one question)', but the inbox has two blueprints and two questions. The listed items add up to 7, not the stated 9. The Clerk's whole job is accurate counts."
   :gdd "§3.1 (Clerk: a count plus a short triage summary)"
   :fix "List the real breakdown: 1 brief, 2 blueprints, 2 questions, 2 chore notices, 1 from Pepper, 1 flagged = 9. Keep each item a one-line summary."}
  {:severity :blocker
   :where ":minutes-ago across :brief-1, :clerk-1, :question-1, :question-2, :chore-1, :chore-2"
   :issue "The timeline doesn't hold together. The brief is 45 minutes old, so now is about 9:00 (the brief pins at 8:15). That puts Sable's question (180) at about 6:00, but the brief says the worker has been stuck 'since 3am'. Vale (20) says 'before I wrap tonight' in the morning. Flora's jar says 'no rush tonight' in the morning. The Clerk (50) counts a brief that arrived after it (45) and misses Vale (20). Biscuit's 'walk hasn't happened yet today' arrives at about 5:00."
   :gdd "§3.1 (morning brief at 8:15); §1 (worker stuck at 3 a.m.)"
   :fix "Anchor 'now' near 9:00. Brief at 45, Clerk at 40 (after everything it counts). Sable at about 360 to match 3am. Vale's question overnight (about 300) with 'before I clock out for the night'. Change Flora's line to 'today's fine'. Send Biscuit's notice at walk time (about 30)."}
  {:severity :blocker
   :where ":brief-1 :body 'stalled around 3am'"
   :issue "'Stalled' is a defined bridge state: an agent that stopped reporting, whose keycards come back. A worker waiting on ask_player under a red '!' is waiting on the player, not stalled. Mixing the two teaches the wrong rule."
   :gdd "§3.1 (ask_player; bridge marks agents stalled when they stop reporting)"
   :fix "'One worker in the storefront workshop hit a question around 3am and is waiting on you under a red !'"}
  {:severity :blocker
   :where ":brief-1 :subject/:body 'three things want you'"
   :issue "The brief misreports what is waiting. It names one blueprint and one stuck worker. It leaves out Fenn's blueprint, Vale's question, and Pepper's signature. That's at least five approvals or answers, plus two chores. The brief's job is to say what waits for approval."
   :gdd "§3.1 (morning brief: one short morning summary); §1 (win the day: every '!' answered)"
   :fix "'Two blueprints on shelves, two questions under a !, one folder for your signature, and Flora and Biscuit want you in person.' Change 'log off' to 'jack out' (see voice)."}
  {:severity :blocker
   :where ":blueprint-1 (Rook, 'canal workshop', canal water shader)"
   :issue "Two problems. First, workshops are one per code repo, and the canal is Flora's home, not a repo. Second, a canal shader is a change to the game's own code, which the author approves as Oracle, not a new player. On their estate a new player approves their own projects."
   :gdd "§2.4 (workshops = repos; canal = Flora's home); §2.8 (author is Oracle only for the game itself)"
   :fix "Point Rook at one of the player's own sample repos, e.g. 'Rook (garden-site workshop): the photo gallery now loads lazily, blueprint's on the shelf.' Update the brief to match."}
  {:severity :blocker
   :where ":blueprint-2 (Fenn) 'Closed out last month's numbers'"
   :issue "Closing the books is Pepper's front-office work, not a worker's task. Workers take a task plus a repo and produce a branch, commits, and a blueprint."
   :gdd "§3.1 (Worker vs Pepper rows)"
   :fix "Make it repo work: 'The ledger app's month-end report now groups by week instead of one lump total. Branch is on the shelf in the ledger workshop.'"}
  {:severity :blocker
   :where ":chore-1 'drifting up to the surface more than usual' / :chore-2 'He's been pacing a little... just waiting on you'"
   :issue "Both notices invent behavior from the real animals that no state reports. The keeper emits only events like 'due :feed' and 'stock 2'. 'Just waiting on you' also leans on guilt."
   :gdd "§3 (twin rule: a twin shows only what its counterpart's real state says); Pillar 1; §3.2 (never shames)"
   :fix "Stick to state and send the player out. Flora: '2 worms left. Next feed is due this evening; pick some up when you're out.' Biscuit: 'Walk's due. The leash is by the door, the real one too. Jack out when you're ready.'"}
  {:severity :blocker
   :where ":pepper-1 'A refund request came in from a customer'"
   :issue "Pepper holds the payments keys. If she read a customer's request from mail, she read raw untrusted content, which only the no-keys Clerk may do. The Clerk's summaries go to the player, never to a keyholder."
   :gdd "§3.2 (a reader with no keys)"
   :fix "Source it from the payments account: 'A refund request opened on the payments account overnight, $12. I drafted the refund and the note to the customer; neither goes out until you sign.'"}
  {:severity :minor
   :where ":bait-1 :from 'security@acme.com' and :clerk-note"
   :issue "The bait comes from the estate's own domain, which looks the same as Hal's and Pepper's real addresses. The Clerk's note also misses a chance to teach the rule. 'Then archiving it' implies a mailbox action from a reader meant only to return counts and summaries."
   :gdd "§3.2 (reader with no keys; identity documents and bank numbers go straight to the provider)"
   :fix "Use a lookalike sender such as 'security@acme-accounts.example'. Note: 'Real providers never ask for a password and a card in one mail. Nothing with keys saw this. Archive it if you like.'"}
  {:severity :minor
   :where ":question-1 and :question-2"
   :issue "Both questions come from the storefront workshop, and both are near-identical A-or-B picks with the same 'either's a small change' line. Both blueprints also end on 'nod and it goes live'. There's little variety for teaching the loop."
   :gdd "§2.4; §1 core loop"
   :fix "Move one question to another workshop and make it open-ended (e.g. 'These rocks all look the same, what feel do you want?'). Give one blueprint a 'redo' path and the other a quick preview line."}
  {:severity :minor
   :where "voice: :brief-1 'Nothing urgent burning... before you log off today', 'the canal water finished rendering'; :pepper-1 'Short one today'; :blueprint-2 'Quick look and a nod'"
   :issue "These are generic office-mail phrasings with no warmth or wry Matrix/TRON shorthand. 'Log off' and 'rendering' aren't the game's words: players jack out, and the change is a shader."
   :gdd "§1 (cast: you're Neo; jack in/out)"
   :fix "Give Hal a companion's touch, e.g. 'Morning. Coffee first, then: ...' and 'Jack out by bedtime and we're square.' Keep it original, with no film lines."}
  {:severity :minor
   :where ":pepper-1 'Folder's on my desk... whenever you pass by'; :question-1 'Standing by at my desk'"
   :issue "These lines don't match the in-world effects. Pepper walks the folder over. A worker with a '!' paces and walks toward you."
   :gdd "§3.1 (Pepper row); §1 (worker paces under a red '!')"
   :fix "'I'll walk the folder over when you're in the lobby.' 'I'll be pacing by my desk.'"}]}